"CUDA 11 Features Revealed | NVIDIA Technical Blog (https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/cuda-11-features-revealed/)\nciteturn9view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/cuda-11-features-revealed/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 375\nL0: 【0†DEVELOPER】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Technical Blog】\nL3: \nL4: [Input: Search blog] [Input] [Input]\nL5: \nL6: 【2†Simulation / Modeling / Design】\nL7: \nL8: [Select]\nL9: \nL10: # CUDA 11 Features Revealed\nL11: \nL12: 【3†Image†developer-blogs.nvidia.com】\nL13: \nL14: May 14, 2020\nL15: \nL16: By 【4†Pramod Ramarao】\nL17: \nL18: [Button: Like ]\nL19: \nL20: 【5†Discuss (4)】\nL21: ## AI-Generated Summary\nL22:   * The 【6†DGX A100†www.nvidia.com】 and 【7†HGX A100†www.nvidia.com】 platforms combine the NVIDIA A100 GPU with NVSwitch fabric for accelerated AI and HPC data centers.\nL23:   * Multi-Instance GPU (MIG) partitions a single A100 into multiple isolated GPU instances, improving utilization for cloud providers and multi-user environments.\nL24:   * Third-generation Tensor Cores on A100 accelerate mixed-precision matrix operations across FP16, Bfloat16, TF32, and FP64 data types.\nL25:   * CUDA 11 introduces L2 cache residency control APIs, asynchronous copy operations, and CUDA Graphs task-graph acceleration to optimize memory access and kernel launch latency on A100.\nL26:   * CUDA libraries including cuBLAS, cuSOLVER, cuTENSOR, cuFFT, and nvJPEG automatically leverage A100 Tensor Cores and hardware JPEG decoding for higher throughput.\nL27:   * Developer tools such as Nsight Compute add Roofline analysis and the Compute Sanitizer replaces cuda-memcheck for memory-access and race-condition checking.\nL28: ### Next Steps\nL29: \nL30:   * Download 【8†CUDA 11】 to begin developing for the NVIDIA Ampere architecture.\nL31:   * Register for the 【9†NVIDIA Developer Program】 to receive updates on CUDA 11 and future releases.\nL32:   * Watch the 【10†Inside the NVIDIA Ampere Architecture†www.nvidia.com】 webinar for a deeper technical dive.\nL33: \nL34: Powered by NVIDIA Nemotron. AI-generated content may summarize information incompletely. Verify important information. 【11†Learn more†www.nvidia.com】\nL35: The new NVIDIA A100 GPU based on the NVIDIA Ampere GPU architecture delivers the greatest generational leap in accelerated computing. The A100 GPU has revolutionary hardware capabilities and we’re excited to announce CUDA 11 in conjunction with A100.\nL36: \nL37: CUDA 11 enables you to leverage the new hardware capabilities to accelerate HPC, genomics, 5G, rendering, deep learning, data analytics, data science, robotics, and many more diverse workloads.\nL38: CUDA 11 is packed full of features—from platform system software to everything that you need to get started and develop GPU-accelerated applications. This post offers an overview of the major software features in this release:\nL39:   * Support for the NVIDIA Ampere GPU architecture, including the new NVIDIA A100 GPU for accelerated scale-up and scale-out of AI and HPC data centers; multi-GPU systems with the NVSwitch fabric such as the 【6†DGX A100†www.nvidia.com】 and 【7†HGX A100†www.nvidia.com】.\nL40:   * Multi-Instance GPU (MIG) partitioning capability that is particularly beneficial to cloud service providers (CSPs) for improved GPU utilization.\nL41:   * New third-generation Tensor Cores to accelerate mixed-precision, matrix operations on different data types, including TF32 and Bfloat16.\nL42:   * Programming and APIs for task graphs, asynchronous data movement, fine-grained synchronization, and L2 cache residency control.\nL43:   * Performance optimizations in CUDA libraries for linear algebra, FFTs, and matrix multiplication.\nL44:   * Updates to the Nsight product family of tools for tracing, profiling, and debugging of CUDA applications.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n5.4. C/C++ Language Extensions — CUDA Programming Guide (https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/05-appendices/cpp-language-extensions.html)\nciteturn9view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/05-appendices/cpp-language-extensions.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 2925\nL0: 【0†Image: country_code†www.nvidia.com】\nL1: \nL2: Back to top\nL3: \nL4: [Input: Search the docs ...] `Ctrl`+`K`\nL5: \nL6: Table of Contents\nL7:   * 【1†CUDA Programming Guide】\nL8:     * 【2†1. Introduction to CUDA】\nL9:       * 【3†1.1. Introduction】\nL10:       * 【4†1.2. Programming Model】\nL11:       * 【5†1.3. The CUDA platform】\nL12:     * 【6†2. Programming GPUs in CUDA】\nL13:       * 【7†2.1. Intro to CUDA C++】\nL14:       * 【8†2.2. Intro to CUDA Python】\nL15:       * 【9†2.3. Writing SIMT Kernels】\nL16:       * 【10†2.4. Writing Tile Kernels】\nL17:       * 【11†2.5. Asynchronous Execution】\nL18:       * 【12†2.6. Unified and System Memory】\nL19:       * 【13†2.7. NVCC: The NVIDIA CUDA Compiler】\nL20:     * 【14†3. Advanced CUDA】\nL21:       * 【15†3.1. Advanced CUDA APIs and Features】\nL22:       * 【16†3.2. Advanced Kernel Programming】\nL23:       * 【17†3.3. The CUDA Driver API】\nL24:       * 【18†3.4. Programming Systems with Multiple GPUs】\nL25:       * 【19†3.5. A Tour of CUDA Features】\nL26:     * 【20†4. CUDA Features】\nL27:       * 【21†4.1. Unified Memory】\nL28:       * 【22†4.2. CUDA Graphs】\nL29:       * 【23†4.3. Stream-Ordered Memory Allocator】\nL30:       * 【24†4.4. Cooperative Groups】\nL31:       * 【25†4.5. Programmatic Dependent Launch and Synchronization】\nL32:       * 【26†4.6. Green Contexts】\nL33:       * 【27†4.7. Locality Domains】\nL34:       * 【28†4.8. Lazy Loading】\nL35:       * 【29†4.9. Error Log Management】\nL36:       * 【30†4.10. Asynchronous Barriers】\nL37:       * 【31†4.11. Pipelines】\nL38:       * 【32†4.12. Asynchronous Data Copies】\nL39:       * 【33†4.13. Work Stealing with Cluster Launch Control】\nL40:       * 【34†4.14. L2 Cache Control】\nL41:       * 【35†4.15. Memory Synchronization Domains】\nL42:       * 【36†4.16. Interprocess Communication】\nL43:       * 【37†4.17. Virtual Memory Management】\nL44:       * 【38†4.18. Compute Fabric Transport】\nL45:       * 【39†4.19. Extended GPU Memory】\nL46:       * 【40†4.20. CUDA Dynamic Parallelism】\nL47:       * 【41†4.21. CUDA Interoperability with APIs】\nL48:       * 【42†4.22. Driver Entry Point Access】\nL49:     * 【43†5. Technical Appendices】\nL50:       * 【44†5.1. Compute Capabilities】\nL51:       * 【45†5.2. CUDA Environment Variables】\nL52:       * 【46†5.3. C++ Language Support】\nL53:       * 【47†5.4. C/C++ Language Extensions】\nL54:       * 【48†5.5. Floating-Point Computation】\nL55:       * 【49†5.6. Device-Callable APIs and Intrinsics】\nL56:       * 【50†5.7. CUDA C++ Memory Model】\nL57:       * 【51†5.8. CUDA C++ Execution model】\nL58:     * 【52†6. Notices】\nL59: # 5.4. C/C++ Language Extensions【53†#】\nL60: \nL61: ## 5.4.1. Function and Variable Annotations【54†#】\nL62: ### 5.4.1.1. Execution Space Specifiers【55†#】\nL63: \nL64: The execution space specifiers `__host__`, `__device__`, `__tile__`, `__global__`, and `__tile_global__` indicate whether a function executes in the host, SIMT, or tile context.\nL65: Table 39 Execution Space Specifier【56†#】\nL66: Execution Space Specifier  | Executed in  | Callable from\nL67: --- | --- | ---\nL68: Host  | SIMT  | Tile  | Host  | SIMT  | Tile\nL69: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL70: `__host__`, no specifier  | ✅  | ❌  | ❌  | ✅  | ❌  | ❌\nL71: `__device__`  | ❌  | ✅  | ❌  | ❌  | ✅  | ❌\nL72: `__global__`  | ❌  | ✅  | ❌  | ✅  | ✅  | ❌\nL73: `__tile__`  | ❌  | ❌  | ✅  | ❌  | ❌  | ✅\nL74: `__tile_global__`  | ❌  | ❌  | ✅  | ✅  | ❌  | ❌\nL75: `__host__ __device__ __tile__`  | ✅  | ✅  | ✅  | ✅  | ✅  | ✅\nL76: \nL77: * * *\nL78: Constraints for `__global__` and `__tile_global__` functions:\nL79: \nL80:   * Must return `void`.\nL81: \nL82:   * Cannot be a member of a `class`, `struct`, or `union`.\nL83: \nL84:   * Requires an execution configuration as described in 【57†Kernel Configuration】.\nL85: \nL86:   * Does not support recursion.\nL87: \nL88:   * Refer to `__global__` 【58†function parameters】 for additional restrictions.\nL89: \nL90: Calls to a `__global__` and `__tile_global__` function are asynchronous. They return to the host thread before the device completes execution.\nL91: \nL92: * * *\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRelease v3.5.0 · NVIDIA/cccl · GitHub (https://github.com/NVIDIA/cccl/releases/tag/v3.5.0)\nciteturn9view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/NVIDIA/cccl/releases/tag/v3.5.0\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 963\nL0: 【0†Skip to content】\nL1: ## Navigation Menu\nL2: \nL3:   * Products\nL4: \nL5:     *       * 【1†GitHub Repository hosting, code review, and collaboration】\nL6:       * 【2†Copilot AI coding agent for tasks, projects and automated work】\nL7: \nL8:     * Build & Ship\nL9:       * 【3†Actions CI/CD and automation】\nL10:       * 【4†Code Quality Quality analysis, fixes, and enforcement】\nL11:       * 【5†Codespaces Cloud development environments】\nL12:     * Secure\nL13:       * 【6†Code Security Vulnerability detection and remediation】\nL14:       * 【7†Secret Protection Secret scanning and leak prevention】\nL15: \nL16: 【8†All Features】\nL17: \nL18:   * Developers\nL19: \nL20:     * Learn\nL21:       * 【9†Documentation†docs.github.com】\nL22:       * 【10†Training†learn.github.com】\nL23:       * 【11†Certifications†learn.github.com】\nL24: \nL25:     * Build\nL26:       * 【12†Build with Copilot】\nL27:       * 【13†Customize Copilot†docs.github.com】\nL28:       * 【14†Integrations】\nL29:     * Explore\nL30:       * 【15†Trending repos】\nL31:       * 【16†GitHub Sponsors】\nL32:       * 【17†Events】\nL33: \nL34:     *       * 【18†Changelog Your source for the latest features, improvements, and fixes across GitHub 